{% set local_problems %}
<p>Arab's location on Brindlee Mountain creates unique drainage challenges that flat-land homeowners simply don't face. When 55+ inches of rain falls each year on hilly terrain, water picks up speed as it flows downhill. If your gutters can't capture and redirect that water, it ends up exactly where you don't want it — at your foundation.</p>

<p>Many older Arab homes were built with sectional gutters that have been leaking for years. The seams separate, debris accumulates at every joint, and water spills over during heavy storms. We've seen homes where decades of improper drainage have caused foundation cracks, basement moisture, and erosion that costs thousands to repair.</p>

<p>Even newer construction in Arab often has undersized or poorly installed gutters. Builders focus on getting homes up quickly and cheaply, not on long-term drainage performance. The result is gutters that overflow, downspouts that deposit water too close to the foundation, and drainage systems that fail when you need them most.</p>
{% endset %}

